Week 3 claim
Claim. For all integers n, if n is even then n2 is even (edited: added explicit
converse note).
Proof. Suppose n is even. Then there exists an integer k such that
n = 2k. Squaring both sides, n2 = (2k)2 = 4k 2 = 2(2k 2 ). Since 2k 2 is an
integer, n2 is of the form 2m for integer m = 2k 2 , so n2 is even. ■
Converse note. The converse (if n2 is even then n is even) also holds,
but proving it requires a separate argument by contrapositive.
Counterexample check. The claim is a conditional. It says nothing
about odd n; testing an odd value such as n = 3 (where n2 = 9 is odd) does
not falsify the claim above.

